all right so let's talk about the  difference between institutional  exchanges and retail exchanges most of  you are probably familiar with retail  crypto exchanges these are places like  coinbase or gemini kraken block fi etc  these exchanges are really great to  onboard people they have super slick  interfaces you can usually trade on a  mobile app and they're a great place for  folks to get started but large  institutions come in and they trade too  much money for these retail exchanges  the fees are too high and there's some  other big technical impediments so what  ends up happening is the institutional  exchanges are where institutions go  trade obviously i'll use an extreme  example somebody like lmax digital now  lmax digital you've probably never never  heard of before the reason why you've  never heard of them before is because  they only serve institutions they're the  number one institutional exchange  and the reason why they only serve  institutions is because it's a different  type of product than that retail product  retail products super slick interface  really built for the individual user the  institutional exchanges usually have a  ton of api support and they're built  specifically to handle large trades high  frequency of volume and they have to be  super super resilient you can't go down  when there's any level of volatility and  that's where something like lmax digital  really thrives so we've got a couple of  charts that we're going to show you that  really highlight the difference between  the retail and institutional market  first here you can see that the total  monthly trading volume and bitcoin  traded has been rising rapidly if you go  all the way back to january 2020 you can  see there was five billion dollars of  monthly trading volume and then all the  way up to 74 billion dollars in may of  21. so really really incredible growth  on something like these institutional  exchanges the next chart that we have  here shows  the  uh three most important factors to  consider when selecting one of these  cryptocurrency trading venues and so you  can see reliability of technology the  number one thing there's plenty of  retail exchanges that people  continuously joke about oh it always  goes down when price moves too much  institutional exchanges can't do that  they have to stay up they also have to  have very deep liquidity if people are  going to come and trade tens of millions  hundreds of millions of dollars at a  time you have to have the liquidity on  the exchange to be able to support that  and then lastly low latency and  certainty of execution what this means  is you can't have a ton of slippage  institutions need to know that if  they're hooking into your api or they're  trying to do high frequency trading that  you're not going to have a ton of  latency and when they say buy or sell  it's actually going to get executed at  the price that they want very very  important for these institutional  exchanges the next one here is  what do you see as the most significant  infrastructure gap in today's crypto  market you can see one access to banking  the institutions are having trouble  getting banking for their digital or  crypto focused trading so that's getting  improved but definitely the number one  thing also access to credit or lack of  global regulation or some of those other  top things that this report put forward  from arcane research and lmax digital  and then next we have  a chart that shows in the next three  years which two segments of the crypto  market will account for the most volumes  traded obviously asset managers and  funds and banks no surprise here they  come into the market with much much  bigger dollars than let's say retail  clients or even corporations so more and  more this market is going to see  institutional participation everyone  from large financial  institutions all the way to the  multi-billion dollar hedge funds that  are coming in really trying to figure  out what is their strategy in this  market and then lastly you can see that  we've got this chart that shows the  average bitcoin trade size by exchange  now this is a fascinating chart again  using the extreme example of lmax  digital only institutional exchange they  don't support retail you can see that  they are head and shoulders above  bitstamp kraken bitflyer coinbase gemini  bitfinex bittrex it bit and binance us  now why are the ace so much bigger it's  because it's only institutions it's a  completely different market and so if we  go and we look at  lmax digital's actual  uh performance year to date they've had  over 350 billion dollars of total traded  volume in the first half of 2021. 350  billion dollars is a big big number and  then you can also see that they're doing  about  2.5 billion dollars of adv and they've  had a record high trading a 6.6 billion  dollars in one single day back in may  and so when you start to see these  institutions coming in it's just a  completely different beast and  understanding the differences is  important one of the key features of  these institutional exchanges that i  think people aren't aware of is not only  that deep liquidity not only that  resilience but also low latency remember  if you think of wall street now most of  the people who are sitting on that  trading floor on wall street at the new  york stock exchange it's more for  performance  art  they're not actually there trading that  much right most of the trading on wall  street is done via computers via apis et  cetera it has been digitized it is now a  battle of the quants it is now a battle  of whose computers can execute trades  faster  that is what is coming to crypto as well  you're going to see this more and more  and so these institutional exchanges  like lmax digital that's their job is to  basically focus on how do we get  reliability on our exchange how do we  consider and make sure every time that  there's high volatility we have high up  time two we 